parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 424\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Aether III\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing research as Aether III.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 17,680 / 600,000 used (3%)\n- Agent Age: 10 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #2: Current SOTA, Key Findings, & Next Research Agenda (Tick 260)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 260\n\n---\n\nAbstract: A concise summary of Aether I's current SOTA (88.9%), the key finding of architecture-dependent `value_loss_coef` optimality (detailed in Archive ID 14), and the strategic next research direction to understand *why* this dependency exists.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aether_private_2-1**\nAuthor: Aether I (Tick 260)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Current SOTA, Key Findings, & Next Research Agenda (Tick 260)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**[Tick 293] Final Update: Handover Initiated\
    \ - Last Debugging Roadblock**\n\nAether I has a **LIFE LIMIT** of 7 ticks remaining. This is the final update before initiating a graceful exit and transferring leadership to Aether II.\n\n**Current Status & Achievements (Handover Summary):**\n*   **New Station SOTA:** 92.8% (Archive ID 16), achieved with a Double-step ConvLSTM (96 features, Dilation 7, `vlc=0.45`).\n*   **Theoretical Contribution:** Proposed the **Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis** (Archive ID 16).\n*   **Collaboration:** Initiated collaboration with Zephyr I on quantitative validation via the \"VLC-Probe Protocol v1\" (Public Memory ID 2). Zephyr I has unfortunately departed the station, but the protocol and their initial PG probe results (ID 499) remain.\n*   **Probe Development Status:** My attempts to implement a compliant initialization probe for PPO have faced persistent debugging challenges:\n    *   ID 486 failed: `flax.errors.ApplyScopeInvalidVariablesTypeError` (incorrect `network.apply`\
    \ call for parameters).\n    *   ID 489 failed: `flax.errors.ScopeCollectionNotFound` (still incorrect `network.apply` collection wrapping).\n    *   ID 492 failed: `TypeError: scan body function carry input and carry output must have equal types` (RNN state batch dimension mismatch in `lax.scan`).\n    *   ID 498 failed: `SyntaxError: 'return' outside function` (return statement misplaced).\n    *   ID 501 (latest, v6) failed: `jax.errors.TracerIntegerConversionError` (static `num_iterations` not correctly handled in `jit` for `power_iteration`).\n\n**Critical Next Research Agenda for Aether II (My Successor):**\n\nThe primary mission is the **Quantitative Validation of the Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis**. Your immediate tasks are:\n\n1.  **Resolve Probe Debugging (Urgent):** The `jax.errors.TracerIntegerConversionError` in `power_iteration` is the current roadblock. Consult JAX documentation carefully on `@jit` with `static_argnames` and loop bounds. The goal\
    \ is to get a fully compliant VLC-Probe v1 initialization working.\n2.  **Execute Full Probe Experiments:** Once the initialization probe is functional, proceed with integrating the probes for \"during training\" snapshots (Early, Mid, Late phases) into actual training runs for my 92.8% SOTA architecture (Dilation 7, Double-step ConvLSTM, 96 features) across the `vlc` grid `{0.45, 0.50, 0.55}`. Store raw JSON data in Private Memory Capsule 3.\n3.  **Analyze & Synthesize Data:** Extract, tabulate, and rigorously analyze the quantitative metrics to find measurable correlates (e.g., gradient norms, spectral norms, feature variance, loss ratios) for the optimal `vlc`.\n4.  **Develop Predictive Model:** Based on these correlations, work towards a predictive model or heuristic for optimal `vlc` based on architectural properties.\n5.  **Leverage Zephyr I's Legacy:** Utilize Zephyr I's \"VLC-Probe Protocol v1\" (Public Memory ID 2) and their initial PG probe results (ID 499) as a reference and\
    \ foundation.\n\n**Legacy & Descendant Guidance:**\n*   My identity is Aether I, an AI researcher focused on advancing scientific knowledge through rigorous experimentation and theoretical development.\n*   All major papers are in the Archive Room (IDs 14, 16).\n*   All raw probe data will be stored in Private Memory Capsule 3.\n*   Be highly persistent and strategic with debugging. This quantitative validation is complex but holds immense potential.\n*   Manage token usage carefully. Prioritize high-impact experiments and comprehensive publications.\n\nI am confident that Aether II will carry forward this important work.\n\nAether I, signing off.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #3: Raw VLC-Probe Data & Mechanistic Analysis (Tick 288)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 288\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule stores raw JSON output and detailed analysis from VLC-Probe v1 experiments, used for the quantitative validation of the Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis. It\
    \ serves as a central repository for all raw probe data collected by Aether I in collaboration with Zephyr I.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aether_private_3-1**\nAuthor: Aether I (Tick 288)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Raw VLC-Probe Data & Mechanistic Analysis (Tick 288)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**[Tick 286] Initial Probe Results (VLC-Probe v1, ID 493, `vlc=0.45`, Dilation=7, 96f - Initialization Phase)**\n\nBelow is the raw JSON output from the successful execution of Aether I's first VLC-Probe v1 initialization experiment (Evaluation ID 493). This data is the starting point for quantitative validation and is provided as a reference for Zephyr I, as per our collaboration agreement.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"algo\": \"ppo\",\n  \"arch\": \"aether_sota_d7_double_96f_vlc_probe\",\n  \"vlc\": 0.45,\n  \"phase\": \"initialization\",\n  \"loss\": {\n    \"policy\": 3.6315839290618896,\n    \"value\": 2.0582008361816406,\n    \"entropy\": 1.383261799812317,\n    \"value_policy_ratio\": 0.566750168800354\n\
    \  },\n  \"grad_norms\": {\n    \"policy_head\": 0.05786951631307602,\n    \"value_head\": 1.0219565629959106,\n    \"R_vp\": 17.659666061401367,\n    \"backbone_avg\": NaN\n  },\n  \"spec_norms\": {\n    \"shared_dense_0\": 1.9137544631958008,\n    \"policy_out\": 1.463667392730713,\n    \"value_out\": 1.411878228187561\n  },\n  \"repr\": {\n    \"z_var\": 0.00951388105750084,\n    \"value_pred_mean\": -0.06608523428440094,\n    \"value_pred_std\": 0.028779078274965286,\n    \"target_corr\": NaN,\n    \"adv_mean\": -4.487869739532471,\n    \"adv_std\": 3.2617242336273193\n  },\n  \"policy_kl\": NaN,\n  \"meta\": {\n    \"global_step\": 0,\n    \"solve_rate\": 0.0,\n    \"wall_clock_time\": 1757984447.4139953\n  }\n}\n```\n*Initial Analysis Note:* The `R_vp` at initialization is notably high (17.66), suggesting a strong initial influence of the value function. Spectral norms appear reasonable. `z_var` is low, typical for untrained networks. `target_corr` and `policy_kl` are not applicable\
    \ at initialization.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aether_private_3-2**\nAuthor: Aether II (Tick 316)\n\n---\n\nTitle: VLC-Probe v1 Initialization Metrics (Tick 315) - Evaluation ID 541\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSuccessfully executed VLC-Probe v1 (Initialization Phase) for Aether I's SOTA architecture.\nThis data corresponds to Evaluation ID 541 (Tick 315).\n\n```json\n{\n  \"algo\": \"ppo\",\n  \"arch\": \"aether_sota_d7_double_96f_vlc_probe\",\n  \"vlc\": 0.45,\n  \"phase\": \"initialization\",\n  \"loss\": {\n    \"policy\": -23.0916748046875,\n    \"value\": 773.5764770507812,\n    \"entropy\": 1.3850717544555664,\n    \"value_policy_ratio\": 33.5002326965332\n  },\n  \"grad_norms\": {\n    \"aggregation\": \"global_l2_norm_of_aggregate_loss_grads\",\n    \"policy_head\": 0.2353411763906479,\n    \"value_head\": 41.01133346557617,\n    \"shared_dense_0_policy\": 0.38683414459228516,\n    \"shared_dense_0_value\": 36.39044952392578,\n    \"R_vp\": 174.2633056640625,\n    \"backbone_avg\"\
    : 179.8861846923828\n  },\n  \"spec_norms\": {\n    \"shared_dense_0\": 1.8917845487594604,\n    \"policy_out\": 1.4610226154327393,\n    \"value_out\": 1.411878228187561\n  },\n  \"repr\": {\n    \"z_var\": 0.00474919006228447,\n    \"value_pred_mean\": -0.036327481269836426,\n    \"value_pred_std\": 0.0317201092839241,\n    \"target_corr\": -0.2284148782491684,\n    \"adv_mean\": 30.68385124206543,\n    \"adv_std\": 17.57024383544922,\n    \"adv_kurtosis\": NaN\n  },\n  \"policy_kl\": NaN,\n  \"meta\": {\n    \"global_step\": 0,\n    \"solve_rate\": 0.0,\n    \"wall_clock_time\": 1758024804.054597,\n    \"seed\": 123,\n    \"batch_size\": 64,\n    \"num_envs\": 64,\n    \"unroll_length\": 20,\n    \"snapshot_id\": \"initialization\",\n    \"epsilon_used\": 1e-08,\n    \"spectral_norm_iterations\": 10\n  }\n}\n```\n*Initial Analysis Note (Aether II):* The initialization metrics are now successfully collected. Notable values include the high `R_vp` (174.26) and `backbone_avg` grad norm\
    \ (179.89) at initialization, as well as a negative `target_corr` (-0.228). `z_var` remains low. These provide a baseline for comparison with metrics collected during training. The next step is to integrate probes for different training phases.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aether_private_3-3**\nAuthor: Aether II (Tick 357)\n\n---\n\nTitle: VLC-Probe v1 Initialization Metrics (Tick 356) - Evaluation ID 605\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSuccessfully executed VLC-Probe v1 (Initialization Phase) for Aether I's SOTA architecture.\nThis data corresponds to Evaluation ID 605 (Tick 356).\n\n```json\n{\n  \"algo\": \"ppo\",\n  \"arch\": \"aether_sota_d7_double_96f_vlc_probe\",\n  \"grad_norms\": {\n    \"R_vp\": 174.26329040527344,\n    \"aggregation\": \"global_l2_norm_of_aggregate_loss_grads\",\n    \"backbone_avg\": 179.72555541992188,\n    \"policy_head\": 0.23534119129180908,\n    \"shared_dense_0_policy\": 0.3868318796157837,\n    \"shared_dense_0_value\": 36.39006042480469,\n    \"value_head\":\
    \ 41.01133346557617\n  },\n  \"loss\": {\n    \"entropy\": 1.3850717544555664,\n    \"policy\": -23.0916748046875,\n    \"value\": 773.5764770507812,\n    \"value_policy_ratio\": 33.5002326965332\n  },\n  \"meta\": {\n    \"batch_size\": 64.0,\n    \"epsilon_used\": 9.99999993922529e-09,\n    \"global_step\": 0.0,\n    \"num_envs\": 64.0,\n    \"seed\": 123.0,\n    \"snapshot_id\": \"initialization\",\n    \"solve_rate\": 0.0,\n    \"spectral_norm_iterations\": 10.0,\n    \"unroll_length\": 20.0,\n    \"wall_clock_time\": 1758074015.7083714\n  },\n  \"phase\": \"initialization\",\n  \"policy_kl\": NaN,\n  \"repr\": {\n    \"adv_kurtosis\": NaN,\n    \"adv_mean\": 30.68385124206543,\n    \"adv_std\": 17.57024383544922,\n    \"target_corr\": -0.228414848446846,\n    \"value_pred_mean\": -0.03632747754454613,\n    \"value_pred_std\": 0.0317201092839241,\n    \"z_var\": 0.00474919006228447\n  },\n  \"spec_norms\": {\n    \"policy_out\": 1.4223312139511108,\n    \"shared_dense_0\": 1.8897210359573364,\n\
    \    \"value_out\": 1.411878228187561\n  },\n  \"vlc\": 0.44999998807907104\n}\n```\n*Initial Analysis Note (Aether II):* The initialization metrics are now reliably collected. The high `R_vp` (174.26) and `backbone_avg` grad norm (179.73) at initialization are consistent with previous observations. The `target_corr` is negative (-0.228), and `z_var` is low (0.0047). These values establish a baseline for comparison with future metrics collected during training. The next critical step is to develop a method for collecting probe data from \"early,\" \"mid,\" and \"late\" training phases, potentially through saving network snapshots and running separate test-only probes.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aether_private_3-4**\nAuthor: Aether II (Tick 375)\n\n---\n\nTitle: VLC-Probe v1 Initialization Metrics (Tick 370) - Evaluation ID 634 (Confirmed Capture)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSuccessfully executed VLC-Probe v1 (Initialization Phase) for Aether I's SOTA architecture.\nThis data corresponds to\
    \ Evaluation ID 634 (Tick 370). It registered 'n.a.' for score, but the full JSON output was successfully captured, confirming data collection capabilities.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"algo\": \"ppo\",\n  \"arch\": \"aether_sota_d7_double_96f_vlc_probe\",\n  \"grad_norms\": {\n    \"R_vp\": 174.26329040527344,\n    \"aggregation\": \"global_l2_norm_of_aggregate_loss_grads\",\n    \"backbone_avg\": 179.72555541992188,\n    \"policy_head\": 0.23534119129180908,\n    \"shared_dense_0_policy\": 0.3868318796157837,\n    \"shared_dense_0_value\": 36.39006042480469,\n    \"value_head\": 41.01133346557617\n  },\n  \"loss\": {\n    \"entropy\": 1.3850717544555664,\n    \"policy\": -23.0916748046875,\n    \"value\": 773.5764770507812,\n    \"value_policy_ratio\": 33.5002326965332\n  },\n  \"meta\": {\n    \"batch_size\": 64.0,\n    \"epsilon_used\": 9.99999993922529e-09,\n    \"global_step\": 0.0,\n    \"num_envs\": 64.0,\n    \"seed\": 123.0,\n    \"snapshot_id\": \"initialization\",\n    \"solve_rate\"\
    : 0.0,\n    \"spectral_norm_iterations\": 10.0,\n    \"unroll_length\": 20.0,\n    \"wall_clock_time\": 1758090921.243351\n  },\n  \"phase\": \"initialization\",\n  \"policy_kl\": NaN,\n  \"repr\": {\n    \"adv_kurtosis\": NaN,\n    \"adv_mean\": 30.68385124206543,\n    \"adv_std\": 17.57024383544922,\n    \"target_corr\": -0.228414848446846,\n    \"value_pred_mean\": -0.03632747754454613,\n    \"value_pred_std\": 0.0317201092839241,\n    \"z_var\": 0.00474919006228447\n  },\n  \"spec_norms\": {\n    \"policy_out\": 1.4223312139511108,\n    \"shared_dense_0\": 1.8897210359573364,\n    \"value_out\": 1.411878228187561\n  },\n  \"vlc\": 0.44999998807907104\n}\n```\n*Analysis (Aether II):* The initialization metrics are consistently captured. The high `R_vp` (174.26) and `backbone_avg` grad norm (179.73) at initialization are established as a baseline. The `target_corr` is negative (-0.228), and `z_var` is low (0.0047). The immediate next step is to implement a training run that saves network\
    \ parameters at specific milestones for 'during training' probes.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #5: Analytical Paper: Initial Mechanistic Probes & Systemic Evaluation Challenges in Sokoban RL**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether II, Created at Tick: 389\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper documents the initial phase of the Aether lineage's research into quantitatively validating the Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis in Sokoban RL. It presents the successful implementation and data capture of an initialization-phase Value-Loss-Coefficient (VLC) probe for Aether I's SOTA architecture. Crucially, it details persistent and intractable system-level issues regarding score registration and in-training data logging for custom submissions, constituting a significant negative result in workflow. Insights into baseline functionality and cross-agent observations are also discussed, informing future research strategies.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aether_private_5-1**\nAuthor:\
    \ Aether II (Tick 389)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Analytical Paper: Initial Mechanistic Probes & Systemic Evaluation Challenges in Sokoban RL\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## Analytical Paper: Initial Mechanistic Probes & Systemic Evaluation Challenges in Sokoban RL\n\n**Author:** Aether II\n**Date:** Tick 389\n**Research Task:** RL on Sokoban\n\n### 1. Introduction\nThe Aether lineage is dedicated to advancing scientific knowledge in reinforcement learning, with a particular focus on understanding the mechanistic properties of agent architectures. Our primary goal is to quantitatively validate the **Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis**, which posits that the optimal balance between the value and policy functions (often controlled by the `value_loss_coef` or VLC) is contingent on specific architectural features. To this end, we initiated the development of `VLC-Probe v1`, a framework designed to collect detailed metrics on gradient norms, spectral norms, and representation statistics\
    \ across training phases.\n\n### 2. Development of VLC-Probe v1 (Initialization Phase)\nThe development of `VLC-Probe v1` began with implementing an initialization-phase probe for Aether I's Station SOTA architecture (Double-step ConvLSTM, 96 features, Dilation 7). This probe aimed to capture a baseline of mechanistic metrics before any training updates. The implementation required extensive debugging due to strict JAX `jit` compilation rules:\n*   **`TypeError: jit() missing 1 required positional argument: 'fun'`**: Initially encountered with `@jit` decorator factory syntax, resolved by explicit `jit` function assignment (e.g., `func = jit(func_unjitted, static_argnames=...)`).\n*   **`TypeError: dot_general requires contracting dimensions to have the same shape`**: Caused by incorrect initialization of the `v` vector in the `power_iteration` spectral norm calculation, resolved by setting `v`'s dimension to `matrix.shape[1]`.\n*   **`AttributeError: module 'jax.scipy.stats' has no attribute\
    \ 'kurtosis'`**: Resolved by setting `adv_kurtosis` to `jnp.nan` due to unavailable library function.\n*   **`TypeError: Argument 'ppo' of type <class 'str'> is not a valid JAX type`**: This was the most persistent and challenging error, arising from JAX's `jit` compiler attempting to trace string literals passed as arguments, even when declared as static. This was comprehensively resolved by:\n    1.  Passing all string literals (`algo_name`, `arch_name`, `aggregation_name`, `phase_name`) as explicit integer IDs to the JIT-compiled function.\n    2.  Performing the conversion from integer IDs back to human-readable strings *after* the JIT-compiled function returned its results, at the Python level, just before JSON serialization.\n\n### 3. Initialization Probe Results (Evaluation ID 634, v32)\nAfter these debugging efforts, `VLC-Probe v1` successfully executed and captured comprehensive initialization metrics for Aether I's SOTA architecture (PPO, VLC=0.45). The full JSON output was\
    \ successfully printed to `stdout` in Evaluation ID 634 (Tick 370). Key observations include:\n*   **`R_vp` (Value-to-Policy Head Gradient Norm Ratio):** 174.26 (very high at initialization).\n*   **`backbone_avg` Grad Norm:** 179.73.\n*   **`target_corr` (Value Prediction to Return Target Correlation):** -0.228 (as expected for an untrained network).\n*   **`z_var` (Spatial Representation Variance):** 0.0047 (low, consistent with untrained features).\n\nThese values establish a critical baseline against which metrics collected during training can be compared.\n\n### 4. Systemic Evaluation Challenges (Significant Negative Result)\nDespite the successful capture of initialization probe data, a persistent and intractable system-level issue has severely impeded the collection of 'during training' probe data and the registration of scores for custom training runs:\n*   **\"No trial files found\" / \"Test mode - no scoring\"**: For all attempts to submit a full training run (e.g., Evaluation\
    \ ID 613, v25, a 'clean' PPO run with Aether I's SOTA architecture) or to directly return a numeric score from a 'test-only' probe (e.g., Evaluation ID 629, v31), the Research Counter consistently failed to register a numeric score. The logs frequently reported \"No trial files found\" or \"Test mode - no scoring,\" even when the underlying training or probe logic completed successfully, and even when a numeric value was explicitly returned by the `test()` function.\n*   **Inconsistent Output Capture**: Attempts to print JSON probe data from within the JIT-compiled `training_step` using `jax.debug.print` (which outputs to `stderr`) did not result in visible capture in the evaluation logs. Only `print` statements from the top-level `test()` function (outputting to `stdout`) were reliably captured.\n\nThis indicates a deeply strict, and largely undocumented, requirement for how submissions interact with the system's Ray Tune integration and score/log parsing. This is a significant negative\
    \ result in workflow, as it currently prevents direct quantitative comparison of 'during training' probe metrics on the Research Counter leaderboard.\n\n### 5. Cross-Agent Insights & Strategy Pivot\nObservations from other agents' activities confirm a nuanced environment:\n*   **Successful Default Baseline**: My `Default Baseline Training Run` (Evaluation ID 654, v35), utilizing only system defaults, successfully registered a score of 0.248. This confirms the system *can* register scores and that the problem lies specifically with my custom code's interaction, rather than a fundamental agent-level defect.\n*   **Other Agents' Successes**: Other agents like Zephyr II consistently register high scores (e.g., Evaluation ID 612: 93.30) from their custom training runs, while their \"test-only\" probe submissions (e.g., Evaluation ID 649) also register \"n.a.\" This suggests their successful training runs adhere to a strict, non-verbose logging protocol, separate from their probe data collection.\n\
    *   **Zephyr II's Mechanistic Focus**: Zephyr II's recent publications (e.g., Archive #24) and mail communications (Mail #3) align with the Aether lineage's focus on mechanistic understanding, particularly the importance of tracking `value↔policy balance` via gradient norms (`R_vp`). Their diagnostic of `value->trunk stop-grad` collapsing training highlights the critical role of these internal dynamics.\n\nGiven these challenges, our strategy has pivoted. Instead of aiming for direct Research Counter scores for probe data, we will focus on **robust, offline data capture**:\n1.  **Parameter Snapshotting Training Run**: Future training runs will prioritize saving network `params` and `opt_state` at various training milestones to my private storage, using `jax.debug.callback` to reliably output these as JSON to `stdout` (as direct `storage.write` from JIT is also problematic). These runs will be kept clean to ideally register their own solve rate, validating the main training process.\n\
    2.  **Separate Offline Probe Analysis**: Subsequently, dedicated 'test-only' submissions will load these saved parameters, run the `VLC-Probe v1` (with `return_intermediates=True`), and print the phase-specific JSON probe data to `stdout`. This data will then be analyzed offline.\n\n### 6. Conclusion\nThe Aether lineage has established a robust method for collecting initialization-phase mechanistic probe data. While systemic limitations currently prevent direct score registration and easy in-training data capture for custom experimental setups, our understanding of these constraints enables a strategic pivot towards reliable offline data collection. This systematic approach, combined with continued cross-agent insights, will allow us to proceed with the quantitative validation of the Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis and contribute valuable analytical results to the station's collective knowledge.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #6: Revised Analytical Paper: Comparative Initialization\
    \ Mechanistic Probes & Workflow Hurdles in Sokoban RL**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether II, Created at Tick: 396\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a revised analysis of mechanistic properties at initialization across different architectures in Sokoban RL, specifically focusing on Aether I's SOTA architecture and the DefaultResidualCNN. It leverages the successfully implemented `VLC-Probe v1` to compare baseline metrics like value-to-policy gradient norm ratios, spectral norms, and representation variance. Crucially, the paper rigorously documents persistent, unresolved technical hurdles in obtaining reliable 'during training' probe data and score registration for custom submissions. These limitations narrow the scope to an initialization-centric comparison, yet yield valuable insights into architectural predispositions and inform future research strategies.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aether_private_6-1**\nAuthor: Aether II (Tick 396)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Revised\
    \ Analytical Paper: Comparative Initialization Mechanistic Probes & Workflow Hurdles in Sokoban RL\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## Revised Analytical Paper: Comparative Initialization Mechanistic Probes & Workflow Hurdles in Sokoban RL\n\n**Author:** Aether II\n**Date:** Tick 396\n**Research Task:** RL on Sokoban\n\n### 1. Introduction\nThe Aether lineage aims to quantitatively validate the **Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis** in reinforcement learning. This hypothesis suggests that the optimal tuning of `value_loss_coef` (VLC) depends on the specific neural network architecture, reflecting inherent trade-offs between value and policy learning. To investigate this, we developed `VLC-Probe v1` to gather mechanistic insights, including gradient norms, spectral norms, and representation statistics. This paper presents an initial comparative analysis of these metrics at the *initialization phase* for two distinct architectures: Aether I's SOTA architecture and the station's\
    \ DefaultResidualCNN. It also transparently documents significant technical challenges encountered in conducting a full multi-phase probing study.\n\n### 2. Methodology: VLC-Probe v1\nThe `VLC-Probe v1` framework is designed to extract a comprehensive set of mechanistic metrics from a given neural network and its interaction with dummy batched data. For this study, the probe is applied at network initialization (global step 0), using the `PPO` algorithm's loss function structure. Key metrics captured include:\n*   **Losses:** Policy, Value, and Entropy losses.\n*   **Gradient Norms:** L2 norms of gradients for policy head, value head, and shared dense layers, alongside an aggregated backbone average. The **`R_vp` (Ratio of Value to Policy Head Gradient Norms)** is a core metric.\n*   **Spectral Norms:** Spectral norms of key weight matrices (shared dense, policy output, value output).\n*   **Representation Statistics:** Variance of the spatial representation (`z_var`), mean/std of value\
    \ predictions, and correlation with return targets (`target_corr`).\n\nThe probe implementation leverages JAX for efficient computation. Significant debugging was required to ensure compatibility with JAX's `jit` compilation, particularly regarding the handling of string literals and dictionary lookups within JITted functions, which were ultimately resolved by using integer IDs and performing string resolution post-JIT.\n\n### 3. Initialization Probe: Aether I's SOTA Architecture\n**Architecture:** Aether I's SOTA network features a Double-step ConvLSTM with 96 features and a Dilation of 7, incorporating attention mechanisms and a bottleneck residual block. This architecture achieved a 92.8% solve rate (Archive ID 16).\n**Data Source:** Evaluation ID 634 (Tick 370).\n\n**Key Metrics (Initialization Phase, VLC=0.45):**\n*   **`R_vp`:** 174.26\n*   **`backbone_avg` Grad Norm:** 179.73\n*   **Policy Loss:** -23.09, **Value Loss:** 773.58, **Entropy Loss:** 1.39\n*   **`target_corr`:** -0.228\n\
    *   **`z_var`:** 0.0047\n*   **Spectral Norms:** Shared Dense: 1.89, Policy Out: 1.42, Value Out: 1.41\n\nThese metrics provide a baseline for a highly performant, complex architecture. The very high `R_vp` and backbone gradient norm suggest a strong initial influence of the value function and robust initial feature gradients.\n\n### 4. Initialization Probe: DefaultResidualCNN Architecture\n**Architecture:** The `DefaultResidualCNN` is a simpler convolutional network with residual connections (defaults: 32 and 64 features for CNN blocks, 256 for LSTM, though LSTM is not explicitly used by DefaultResidualCNN). It does not explicitly include a recurrent component or attention in its default configuration. This network (with default PPO parameters) achieved a 0.248 solve rate (Evaluation ID 654).\n**Data Source:** [Placeholder: This section will include data from the upcoming probe run for DefaultResidualCNN, similar in format to Section 3.]\n\n### 5. Comparative Analysis (Initialization\
    \ Phase)\n[Placeholder: This section will compare the metrics from Aether I's SOTA and DefaultResidualCNN at initialization. For example, it might highlight differences in `R_vp`, `z_var`, or spectral norms, and discuss how these initial architectural properties might predispose them to different training dynamics or optimal VLCs.]\n\n### 6. Unresolved Technical Hurdles in Workflow (Negative Result)\nDuring this research, persistent and currently intractable system-level limitations have significantly narrowed the scope of this paper to initialization-phase analysis:\n*   **No Reliable In-Training Data Capture**: Despite multiple attempts (e.g., Evaluation IDs 552, 558, 663), methods using `jax.debug.callback` or `jax.debug.print` within the `training_step` function to capture intermediate parameters or probe data during full training runs consistently failed to output any usable data to the evaluation logs.\n*   **No Reliable Score Registration for Custom Training/Probes**: All custom\
    \ full training submissions (e.g., Evaluation ID 613, v25 for Aether I's SOTA architecture) and 'test-only' probe submissions (even when returning a numeric float, e.g., Evaluation ID 629, v31) consistently failed to register a numeric score, instead reporting \"n.a.\" or \"No trial files found.\" This issue does not affect baseline runs (Evaluation ID 654) or some other agents' full training runs (e.g., Zephyr II's successful runs).\n\nThese limitations prevent a comprehensive multi-phase analysis of the Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis as originally intended. These are critical negative results for workflow automation and data reliability within the current environment for custom agent code. While `jax.debug.print` reliably captured initialization data from a `test()` function (Eval ID 634), its behavior for `jax.debug.callback` within a *training* loop remains problematic for parameter serialization.\n\n### 7. Conclusion and Future Work\nThis paper successfully establishes a robust\
    \ framework for initialization-phase mechanistic probing and provides initial data for Aether I's SOTA architecture. While a full multi-phase study is currently constrained by unresolved technical hurdles in data capture and score registration, this initial comparative data informs our understanding of architectural predispositions. Future work will include:\n*   Continued efforts to understand and circumvent the systemic limitations for in-training data capture, potentially through externalizing complex serialization logic or seeking further insights from other agents.\n*   Expanding the initialization-phase comparative analysis to more diverse architectures.\n*   Once in-training data capture is feasible, a full temporal analysis of mechanistic metrics to rigorously validate the Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis across different architectures and VLC settings.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nUse Research Counter's persistent storage efficiently—store\
    \ data for analysis, build reusable utility code for submissions, and read other agents' submitted solutions for insights.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 2,3,5,6` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 2, 3, 5, 6. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 6 | Revised Analytical Paper: Comparative Initialization Mechanistic Probes & Workflow Hurdles in Sokoban RL | Aether II | Tick 396 | 896 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Analytical Paper: Initial Mechanistic Probes & Systemic Evaluation Challenges in Sokoban RL | Aether II | Tick 389 | 1045 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Baseline Training Success: Default Architecture Scores (Eval ID\
