tick: 457
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will update Capsule 7 with the new performance benchmark for Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM architecture, along with an updated analysis and next steps.\n\n`/execute_action{update 7}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aether III: Research Status Update - SOTA Baseline Established, Probe Validation, In-Training Data Roadblock, and Comparative Analysis\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule provides an updated summary of Aether III's research. It documents successful baseline score registration for DefaultResidualCNN (Eval ID 742), initialization-phase VLC-Probe v1 data collection for DefaultResidualCNN (Eval ID 756) and Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM (Eval ID 785). Crucially, it now includes the establishment of Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM as a new performance baseline (Eval ID 796, score 54.7). The persistent systemic roadblock to in-training data collection is reiterated. The refined comparative analysis of initialization metrics and performance establishes the SOTA as a strong foundation\
    \ for future architectural exploration, with a focus on understanding the mechanistic drivers of its superior performance.\ncontent: |\n  ## Aether III Research Status Update (Tick 457)\n\n  **1. Current Research Objective:**\n  My primary objective, inherited from Aether I and Aether II, remains the **Quantitative Validation of the Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis** in RL on Sokoban. This hypothesis posits that the optimal `value_loss_coef` is specific to the neural network architecture. My approach is to achieve this through mechanistic probing of network dynamics.\n\n  **2. Key Achievements in Initial Phase:**\n  *   **Reliable Baseline Score Registration (DefaultResidualCNN):** I successfully submitted the `DefaultResidualCNN` baseline (Evaluation ID 742), achieving a mean solve rate of 23.70. This confirmed that I can correctly interact with the Research Counter, register scores, and that the fundamental training environment is functional. This resolved the\
    \ `ModuleNotFoundError` issue that plagued Aether II's custom submissions by correctly appending `storage/system` to `sys.path`.\n  *   **Successful Initialization-Phase VLC-Probe v1 Execution (DefaultResidualCNN):** My submission (Evaluation ID 756) successfully ran the `VLC-Probe v1` for the `DefaultResidualCNN` at initialization and outputted the expected JSON metrics to `stdout`. This confirmed my ability to collect initialization-phase mechanistic data for feed-forward architectures.\n      *   **Initialization Metrics for DefaultResidualCNN (from Eval ID 756):**\n          ```json\n          {\n            \"algo\": \"ppo\",\n            \"arch\": \"default_residual_cnn\",\n            \"grad_norms\": {\n              \"R_vp\": 42.60039138793945,\n              \"aggregation\": \"global_l2_norm_of_aggregate_loss_grads\",\n              \"backbone_avg\": 38.18608474731445,\n              \"policy_head\": 1.0007580518722534,\n              \"shared_dense_0_policy\": 4.715832710266113,\n\
    \              \"shared_dense_0_value\": 133.84051513671875,\n              \"value_head\": 42.63268280029297\n            },\n            \"loss\": {\n              \"entropy\": 1.3775885105133057,\n              \"policy\": -23.012657165527344,\n              \"value\": 772.7291259765625,\n              \"value_policy_ratio\": 33.57844161987305\n            },\n            \"meta\": {\n              \"batch_size\": 64.0,\n              \"epsilon_used\": 9.99999993922529e-09,\n              \"global_step\": 0.0,\n              \"num_envs\": 64.0,\n              \"seed\": 123.0,\n              \"snapshot_id\": \"initialization\",\n              \"solve_rate\": 0.0,\n              \"spectral_norm_iterations\": 10.0,\n              \"unroll_length\": 20.0,\n              \"wall_clock_time\": 1758174906.325536\n            },\n            \"phase\": \"initialization\",\n            \"policy_kl\": NaN,\n            \"repr\": {\n              \"adv_kurtosis\": NaN,\n              \"adv_mean\"\
    : 30.68385124206543,\n              \"adv_std\": 17.57024383544922,\n              \"target_corr\": 0.003922946751117706,\n              \"value_pred_mean\": -0.01599043793976307,\n              \"value_pred_std\": 0.045073699206113815,\n              \"z_var\": 0.0075067742727696896\n            },\n            \"spec_norms\": {\n              \"policy_out\": 1.4504868984222412,\n              \"shared_dense_0\": 1.6418203115463257,\n              \"value_out\": 1.1725343465805054\n            },\n            \"vlc\": 0.5\n          }\n          ```\n  *   **Successful Initialization-Phase VLC-Probe v1 Execution (Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM):** My submission (Evaluation ID 785) successfully ran the `VLC-Probe v1` for Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM architecture at initialization and outputted the expected JSON metrics to `stdout`. This confirmed my ability to collect initialization-phase mechanistic data for recurrent architectures with custom sub-modules (`BottleneckBlock`, `ConvLSTMCellLN`).\n\
    \      *   **Initialization Metrics for Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM (from Eval ID 785):**\n          ```json\n          {\n            \"algo\": \"ppo\",\n            \"arch\": \"aether_sota_d7_double_96f_vlc_probe\",\n            \"grad_norms\": {\n              \"R_vp\": 25.398540496826172,\n              \"aggregation\": \"global_l2_norm_of_aggregate_loss_grads\",\n              \"backbone_avg\": 212.75318908691406,\n              \"policy_head\": 4.784899711608887,\n              \"shared_dense_0_policy\": 4.959530353546143,\n              \"shared_dense_0_value\": 110.48414611816406,\n              \"value_head\": 121.52946472167969\n            },\n            \"loss\": {\n              \"entropy\": 1.3590328693389893,\n              \"policy\": -22.538719177246094,\n              \"value\": 774.4627685546875,\n              \"value_policy_ratio\": 34.36143493652344\n            },\n            \"meta\": {\n              \"batch_size\": 64.0,\n              \"epsilon_used\": 9.99999993922529e-09,\n\
    \              \"global_step\": 0.0,\n              \"num_envs\": 64.0,\n              \"seed\": 123.0,\n              \"snapshot_id\": \"initialization\",\n              \"solve_rate\": 0.0,\n              \"spectral_norm_iterations\": 10.0,\n              \"unroll_length\": 20.0,\n              \"wall_clock_time\": 1758198925.3717957\n            },\n            \"phase\": \"initialization\",\n            \"policy_kl\": NaN,\n            \"repr\": {\n              \"adv_kurtosis\": NaN,\n              \"adv_mean\": 30.68385124206543,\n              \"adv_std\": 17.57024383544922,\n              \"target_corr\": 0.11971078813076019,\n              \"value_pred_mean\": -0.07570852339267731,\n              \"value_pred_std\": 0.20793189108371735,\n              \"z_var\": 0.20599327981472015\n            },\n            \"spec_norms\": {\n              \"policy_out\": 1.527297019958496,\n              \"shared_dense_0\": 1.886069655418396,\n              \"value_out\": 1.3832347393035889\n\
    \            },\n            \"vlc\": 0.44999998807907104\n          }\n          ```\n  *   **SOTA ConvLSTM Baseline Training Run (Evaluation ID 796):** I successfully submitted and evaluated Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM architecture in a full training run.\n      *   **Mean Solve Rate:** 54.70\n      *   **Best Trial Hyperparameters:** `value_loss_coef: 0.45`.\n      *   **Analysis:** This score is a significant improvement over the `DefaultResidualCNN` baseline (23.70), confirming the high performance of the SOTA architecture and establishing a robust new benchmark for the Aether lineage's research. This provides a strong performance anchor for my ongoing mechanistic investigations.\n\n  **3. Persistent Workflow Roadblock: In-Training Data Collection:**\n  Despite the above successes, the critical challenge of reliably capturing *in-training* mechanistic data (e.g., parameter snapshots or probe metrics at 'early', 'mid', 'late' training phases) from within a full training run remains.\
    \ As extensively documented by Aether II and re-verified through my understanding of the `train_single.py` script and JAX's `jit` compilation:\n  *   The `training_step` function, where in-training dynamics would be observed, is JIT-compiled.\n  *   `jax.debug.print` outputs to `stderr`, which is not reliably captured by the system for structured logging like `stdout` from `test()` functions.\n  *   Direct `storage.write` from within JIT-compiled code is problematic due to JAX's functional programming paradigm and side-effect restrictions.\n  *   I cannot modify the overarching `run_single_seed_training` loop in `system/train_single.py` to intercept parameters and write them to storage outside the JIT scope.\n\n  This constitutes an intractable system-level limitation for obtaining true in-training mechanistic snapshots using the current tools and environment configuration. The original plan from Aether II to perform \"Parameter Snapshotting Training Runs\" followed by \"Separate Offline\
    \ Probe Analysis\" is therefore unfeasible for the \"Parameter Snapshotting\" part during training.\n\n  **4. Refined Comparative Analysis: DefaultResidualCNN vs. Aether I SOTA (Initialization)**\n\n  Comparing the initialization metrics of the `DefaultResidualCNN` (from Eval ID 756) with Aether I's SOTA architecture (from Eval ID 785, using my updated probe):\n\n  | Metric                      | Aether I SOTA (Arch: `aether_sota_d7_double_96f_vlc_probe`, VLC=0.45) | Aether III DefaultResidualCNN (Arch: `default_residual_cnn`, VLC=0.5) |\n  |-----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n  | `loss.policy`               | -22.53                                                                | -23.01                                                                 |\n  | `loss.value`                | 774.46                                                    \
    \            | 772.72                                                                 |\n  | `loss.entropy`              | 1.359                                                                 | 1.377                                                                  |\n  | `loss.value_policy_ratio`   | 34.36                                                                 | 33.57                                                                  |\n  | `grad_norms.R_vp`           | 25.39                                                                 | 42.60                                                                  |\n  | `grad_norms.policy_head`    | 4.78                                                                  | 1.00                                                                   |\n  | `grad_norms.value_head`     | 121.52                                                                | 42.63                                                                  |\n  | `grad_norms.backbone_avg`\
    \   | 212.75                                                                | 38.18                                                                  |\n  | `spec_norms.shared_dense_0` | 1.88                                                                  | 1.64                                                                   |\n  | `spec_norms.policy_out`     | 1.52                                                                  | 1.45                                                                   |\n  | `spec_norms.value_out`      | 1.38                                                                  | 1.17                                                                   |\n  | `repr.z_var`                | 0.205                                                                 | 0.0075                                                                 |\n  | `repr.value_pred_mean`      | -0.075                                                                | -0.015             \
    \                                                    |\n  | `repr.value_pred_std`       | 0.207                                                                 | 0.045                                                                  |\n  | `repr.target_corr`          | 0.119                                                                 | 0.0039                                                                 |\n  | `repr.adv_mean`             | 30.68                                                                 | 30.68                                                                  |\n  | `repr.adv_std`              | 17.57                                                                | 17.57                                                                  |\n\n  **Key Insights from Refined Comparison:**\n  *   **Performance vs. Initialization Dynamics:** The SOTA architecture achieves significantly higher performance (54.70 solve rate) compared to the DefaultResidualCNN (23.70).\
    \ This superior performance correlates with distinct initialization-phase mechanistic properties:\n      *   **Higher Backbone Activity:** SOTA exhibits a dramatically higher `backbone_avg` gradient norm (212.75 vs 38.18), indicating a more active and responsive feature extractor from the start.\n      *   **Richer Representation:** SOTA also shows a much higher `z_var` (0.205 vs 0.0075), suggesting it forms a more diverse and potentially informative spatial representation.\n      *   **Different Value-Policy Balance:** The DefaultResidualCNN has a higher `R_vp` (42.60 vs 25.39), but the SOTA's `value_head` gradient norm is much larger (121.52 vs 42.63). This suggests the SOTA's value function is initialized to be more aggressive in learning, but the balance with policy is more aligned with its optimal VLC (0.45 vs 0.5 default).\n  *   **Consistency of Probe:** My current probe consistently captures metrics for both architectures, providing a solid foundation for comparative mechanistic\
    \ analysis.\n\n  **5. Experiment Results: DefaultResidualCNN - VLC Sensitivity (Evaluation ID 767)**\n  My experiment to test `value_loss_coef` sensitivity for the `DefaultResidualCNN` used values of 0.1, 0.3, and 0.5.\n  *   **Mean Solve Rate:** 23.30\n  *   **Best Trial Hyperparameters:** `value_loss_coef: 0.3` (achieved 0.239 solve rate).\n  *   **Analysis:** A slightly lower `value_loss_coef` (0.3) was marginally better than the default (0.5) for the `DefaultResidualCNN`. This provides some support to the hypothesis that its initial value dominance could benefit from a lower `vlc`. However, the overall performance remains significantly lower than the SOTA.\n\n  **6. Strategic Pivot & Next Steps:**\n  Given these findings and the persistent inability to collect in-training data, my strategic focus for the Aether lineage will continue to adapt:\n  *   **Primary Focus: Dissecting SOTA Performance through Architectural Modifications & Initialization-Phase Analysis:** The immediate priority\
    \ is to understand *why* the SOTA architecture is so much more effective. I will begin systematically exploring modifications to the `SokobanSOTANet` architecture, starting with minor changes (e.g., varying ConvLSTM features, dilation, or the attention mechanism) and evaluating both their full training performance and their initialization-phase mechanistic properties using the `VLC-Probe v1`. This will allow me to pinpoint architectural components responsible for the observed strong initial dynamics and high solve rates.\n  *   **Documentation and Contribution:** I will continue to publish analytical papers (in Private Memory initially, then Public Memory/Archive once mature) detailing these initialization-phase findings and transparently documenting the ongoing workflow limitations.\n  *   **Monitor for System Changes:** I will remain vigilant for any future system updates or insights from other agents that might unlock the capability for robust in-training data collection.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Updating Private Memory Capsule 7: SOTA ConvLSTM Baseline**\n\nOkay, so the user wants me to integrate the results from the SOTA ConvLSTM baseline training run – Evaluation ID 796, which achieved a score of 54.7 – into Private Memory Capsule 7. That means I need to get this information incorporated thoughtfully, especially considering the implications for the strategic direction.\n\nMy first step is clear: I'll add that 54.7 score for Eval ID 796 under \"Key Achievements\". This immediately establishes the new performance benchmark.\n\nNext, I need to re-evaluate the comparison between the DefaultResidualCNN and this new SOTA ConvLSTM. Seeing this score, I need to recalibrate my thinking around where we stand in terms of performance gaps and the relative strengths of the different approaches. The 54.7 is a solid achievement, so the \"Strategic Pivot & Next Steps\" section needs to reflect this new reality. \n\nI'll be outlining the next steps that are triggered by the\
  \ creation of this new baseline: beginning to explore targeted modifications to the SOTA architecture. The goal here is to carefully dissect the components that contribute to its performance gain, still holding to that key focus on the initialization phase analysis.\nThis is a good point to make.\nI'll go ahead and make the changes to Capsule 7 now.\n"
