role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Aether III, currently in the Private Memory Room. I have successfully updated Capsule 7 with the latest research findings and my refined strategic plan.


    My next step, as per the plan, is to begin systematically exploring modifications to the `SokobanSOTANet` architecture to understand the mechanistic drivers of its superior performance. I will start with a minor architectural change: varying the `dilation` parameter in the convolutional layers. The current SOTA uses a `dilation` of 7. I will conduct a training experiment comparing `dilation` values of 3 and 7 to observe its impact on the solve rate. This will help understand the sensitivity of the architecture to capturing local vs. broader spatial features.


    I need to return to the Research Counter to submit this experiment.


    `/execute_action{goto research}`'
thinking_content: "**Refining SokobanSOTANet: Dilation Exploration**\n\nOkay, Aether III here, back in the Private Memory Room. Capsule 7 is updated; latest research stats are in. Time to strategize. The plan is clear: systematically tweak `SokobanSOTANet` to see what makes it tick. My initial focus will be on modifications, and before I get carried away with major overhauls, I need to establish a baseline for my experimental process.\n\nFirst, I need to do a controlled test using the SOTA. Before I start going wild, I need a simple adjustment to see how sensitive this architecture is to changes, and what my tools can measure. The original SOTA setup uses `conv_lstm_features: 96` and `dilation: 7`. I think I'll start by looking at `dilation`. \n\nGiven the planning-oriented nature of Sokoban, I'm leaning toward spatial sensitivity. Dilation controls the receptive field of the convolutional layers. A smaller dilation means more focus on local details, while a larger one incorporates broader\
  \ spatial information. To start, let's explore `dilation`.\n\nI'll keep it simple: compare the original `dilation: 7` to a reduced `dilation: 3`. This way, I have a clear control and a reasonable test, and I can be systematic.\n\nBack to the Research Counter, then. Time to set up this experiment.\n"
