- text: 'My current experiment, Evaluation ID 811, testing the SOTA ConvLSTM''s sensitivity to the number of `conv_lstm_features`, is currently running. I will await its completion to understand the impact of this architectural feature on the solve rate.


    Once the results are available, I will analyze the performance of different feature counts and update my research status in Private Memory Capsule 7 accordingly. After that, having gained maturity and observed the high scores from other agents like Zephyr II, I will begin to assimilate external knowledge thoughtfully and critically, investigating their successful approaches.'
thinking_content: '**Analyzing ConvLSTM Sensitivity and Planning the Next Steps**


  Okay, so I''ve just kicked off experiment Eval ID 811. This is focused on the SOTA ConvLSTM and how it behaves with different numbers of `conv_lstm_features`. Now, the only thing I can do is wait for those results to come in. Once they do, I''ve got a clear plan: first, a detailed analysis of the performance across those different values. Then, I need to update Private Memory Capsule 7 with this fresh data. Finally, and this is crucial, I''ll use those findings to decide my next move: either I dive into another architectural tweak or, considering I''m now mature (51 ticks!), maybe it''s time to start seriously evaluating what other agents are doing.


  The system message after maturity was pretty clear - don''t jump ship just because others have higher scores, but do be smart about incorporating new knowledge. It also nudged me to apply my lineage''s "novel method," the `VLC-Probe v1` and the architecture-dependent VLC hypothesis, to SOTA scripts. I''ve been doing that, obviously, with the baseline and SOTA.


  But the Research Counter is showing Zephyr II and Krono IV in the 90s! Their scores are significantly better than the 54.70 I managed with Aether I''s SOTA. This is a massive indication that there are fundamentally better architectures or approaches out there. It''s time to start exploring their work, but not yet. The *immediate* next action is to get the results of Eval ID 811.

  '
