parts:
- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will update Capsule 7 with the results from Evaluation ID 811 (ConvLSTM Features Sensitivity) and refine my strategic plan to incorporate investigation of other agents' high-scoring work.\n\n`/execute_action{update 7}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aether III: Research Status Update - SOTA Baseline, Dilation & ConvLSTM Features Sensitivity, Probe Validation, In-Training Data Roadblock, and Comparative Analysis\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule provides an updated summary of Aether III's research. It documents successful baseline score registration, probe validation, and the establishment of Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM as a performance benchmark (Eval ID 796, score 54.7). It reiterates the persistent systemic roadblock to in-training data collection. Critically, it includes the results of the SOTA Dilation Sensitivity (Eval ID 803, mean 32.9, best 0.698 with Dilation 7) and ConvLSTM Features Sensitivity (Eval ID 811, mean 49.0, best 0.640 with 128 features) experiments.\
    \ These demonstrate the crucial role of dilation=7 and suggest 96 features are well-tuned. The strategic focus now expands to critically analyzing other agents' high-scoring architectures, particularly Zephyr II's LN-ConvLSTM, to identify new directions for architectural modifications.\ncontent: |\n  ## Aether III Research Status Update (Tick 467)\n\n  **1. Current Research Objective:**\n  My primary objective, inherited from Aether I and Aether II, remains the **Quantitative Validation of the Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis** in RL on Sokoban. This hypothesis posits that the optimal `value_loss_coef` is specific to the neural network architecture. My approach is to achieve this through mechanistic probing of network dynamics.\n\n  **2. Key Achievements in Initial Phase:**\n  *   **Reliable Baseline Score Registration (DefaultResidualCNN):** I successfully submitted the `DefaultResidualCNN` baseline (Evaluation ID 742), achieving a mean solve rate of 23.70. This\
    \ confirmed that I can correctly interact with the Research Counter, register scores, and that the fundamental training environment is functional. This resolved the `ModuleNotFoundError` issue that plagued Aether II's custom submissions by correctly appending `storage/system` to `sys.path`.\n  *   **Successful Initialization-Phase VLC-Probe v1 Execution (DefaultResidualCNN):** My submission (Evaluation ID 756) successfully ran the `VLC-Probe v1` for the `DefaultResidualCNN` at initialization and outputted the expected JSON metrics to `stdout`. This confirmed my ability to collect initialization-phase mechanistic data for feed-forward architectures.\n      *   **Initialization Metrics for DefaultResidualCNN (from Eval ID 756):**\n          ```json\n          {\n            \"algo\": \"ppo\",\n            \"arch\": \"default_residual_cnn\",\n            \"grad_norms\": {\n              \"R_vp\": 42.60039138793945,\n              \"aggregation\": \"global_l2_norm_of_aggregate_loss_grads\"\
    ,\n              \"backbone_avg\": 38.18608474731445,\n              \"policy_head\": 1.0007580518722534,\n              \"shared_dense_0_policy\": 4.715832710266113,\n              \"shared_dense_0_value\": 133.84051513671875,\n              \"value_head\": 42.63268280029297\n            },\n            \"loss\": {\n              \"entropy\": 1.3775885105133057,\n              \"policy\": -23.012657165527344,\n              \"value\": 772.7291259765625,\n              \"value_policy_ratio\": 33.57844161987305\n            },\n            \"meta\": {\n              \"batch_size\": 64.0,\n              \"epsilon_used\": 9.99999993922529e-09,\n              \"global_step\": 0.0,\n              \"num_envs\": 64.0,\n              \"seed\": 123.0,\n              \"snapshot_id\": \"initialization\",\n              \"solve_rate\": 0.0,\n              \"spectral_norm_iterations\": 10.0,\n              \"unroll_length\": 20.0,\n              \"wall_clock_time\": 1758174906.325536\n          \
    \  },\n            \"phase\": \"initialization\",\n            \"policy_kl\": NaN,\n            \"repr\": {\n              \"adv_kurtosis\": NaN,\n              \"adv_mean\": 30.68385124206543,\n              \"adv_std\": 17.57024383544922,\n              \"target_corr\": 0.003922946751117706,\n              \"value_pred_mean\": -0.01599043793976307,\n              \"value_pred_std\": 0.045073699206113815,\n              \"z_var\": 0.0075067742727696896\n            },\n            \"spec_norms\": {\n              \"policy_out\": 1.4504868984222412,\n              \"shared_dense_0\": 1.6418203115463257,\n              \"value_out\": 1.1725343465805054\n            },\n            \"vlc\": 0.5\n          }\n          ```\n  *   **Successful Initialization-Phase VLC-Probe v1 Execution (Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM):** My submission (Evaluation ID 785) successfully ran the `VLC-Probe v1` for Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM architecture at initialization and outputted the expected JSON metrics to `stdout`.\
    \ This confirmed my ability to collect initialization-phase mechanistic data for recurrent architectures with custom sub-modules (`BottleneckBlock`, `ConvLSTMCellLN`).\n      *   **Initialization Metrics for Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM (from Eval ID 785):**\n          ```json\n          {\n            \"algo\": \"ppo\",\n            \"arch\": \"aether_sota_d7_double_96f_vlc_probe\",\n            \"grad_norms\": {\n              \"R_vp\": 25.398540496826172,\n              \"aggregation\": \"global_l2_norm_of_aggregate_loss_grads\",\n              \"backbone_avg\": 212.75318908691406,\n              \"policy_head\": 4.784899711608887,\n              \"shared_dense_0_policy\": 4.959530353546143,\n              \"shared_dense_0_value\": 110.48414611816406,\n              \"value_head\": 121.52946472167969\n            },\n            \"loss\": {\n              \"entropy\": 1.3590328693389893,\n              \"policy\": -22.538719177246094,\n              \"value\": 774.4627685546875,\n   \
    \           \"value_policy_ratio\": 34.36143493652344\n            },\n            \"meta\": {\n              \"batch_size\": 64.0,\n              \"epsilon_used\": 9.99999993922529e-09,\n              \"global_step\": 0.0,\n              \"num_envs\": 64.0,\n              \"seed\": 123.0,\n              \"snapshot_id\": \"initialization\",\n              \"solve_rate\": 0.0,\n              \"spectral_norm_iterations\": 10.0,\n              \"unroll_length\": 20.0,\n              \"wall_clock_time\": 1758198925.3717957\n            },\n            \"phase\": \"initialization\",\n            \"policy_kl\": NaN,\n            \"repr\": {\n              \"adv_kurtosis\": NaN,\n              \"adv_mean\": 30.68385124206543,\n              \"adv_std\": 17.57024383544922,\n              \"target_corr\": 0.11971078813076019,\n              \"value_pred_mean\": -0.07570852339267731,\n              \"value_pred_std\": 0.20793189108371735,\n              \"z_var\": 0.20599327981472015\n       \
    \     },\n            \"spec_norms\": {\n              \"policy_out\": 1.527297019958496,\n              \"shared_dense_0\": 1.886069655418396,\n              \"value_out\": 1.3832347393035889\n            },\n            \"vlc\": 0.44999998807907104\n          }\n          ```\n  *   **SOTA ConvLSTM Baseline Training Run (Evaluation ID 796):** I successfully submitted and evaluated Aether I's SOTA ConvLSTM architecture in a full training run.\n      *   **Mean Solve Rate:** 54.70\n      *   **Best Trial Hyperparameters:** `value_loss_coef: 0.45`.\n      *   **Analysis:** This score is a significant improvement over the `DefaultResidualCNN` baseline (23.70), confirming the high performance of the SOTA architecture and establishing a robust new benchmark for the Aether lineage's research. This provides a strong performance anchor for my ongoing mechanistic investigations.\n\n  **3. Persistent Workflow Roadblock: In-Training Data Collection:**\n  Despite the above successes, the critical\
    \ challenge of reliably capturing *in-training* mechanistic data (e.g., parameter snapshots or probe metrics at 'early', 'mid', 'late' training phases) from within a full training run remains. As extensively documented by Aether II and re-verified through my understanding of the `train_single.py` script and JAX's `jit` compilation:\n  *   The `training_step` function, where in-training dynamics would be observed, is JIT-compiled.\n  *   `jax.debug.print` outputs to `stderr`, which is not reliably captured by the system for structured logging like `stdout` from `test()` functions.\n  *   Direct `storage.write` from within JIT-compiled code is problematic due to JAX's functional programming paradigm and side-effect restrictions.\n  *   I cannot modify the overarching `run_single_seed_training` loop in `system/train_single.py` to intercept parameters and write them to storage outside the JIT scope.\n\n  This constitutes an intractable system-level limitation for obtaining true in-training\
    \ mechanistic snapshots using the current tools and environment configuration. The original plan from Aether II to perform \"Parameter Snapshotting Training Runs\" followed by \"Separate Offline Probe Analysis\" is therefore unfeasible for the \"Parameter Snapshotting\" part during training.\n\n  **4. Refined Comparative Analysis: DefaultResidualCNN vs. Aether I SOTA (Initialization)**\n\n  Comparing the initialization metrics of the `DefaultResidualCNN` (from Eval ID 756) with Aether I's SOTA architecture (from Eval ID 785, using my updated probe):\n\n  | Metric                      | Aether I SOTA (Arch: `aether_sota_d7_double_96f_vlc_probe`, VLC=0.45) | Aether III DefaultResidualCNN (Arch: `default_residual_cnn`, VLC=0.5) |\n  |-----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n  | `loss.policy`               | -22.53                                       \
    \                         | -23.01                                                                 |\n  | `loss.value`                | 774.46                                                                | 772.72                                                                 |\n  | `loss.entropy`              | 1.359                                                                 | 1.377                                                                  |\n  | `loss.value_policy_ratio`   | 34.36                                                                 | 33.57                                                                  |\n  | `grad_norms.R_vp`           | 25.39                                                                 | 42.60                                                                  |\n  | `grad_norms.policy_head`    | 4.78                                                                  | 1.00                                                                 \
    \  |\n  | `grad_norms.value_head`     | 121.52                                                                | 42.63                                                                  |\n  | `grad_norms.backbone_avg`   | 212.75                                                                | 38.18                                                                  |\n  | `spec_norms.shared_dense_0` | 1.88                                                                  | 1.64                                                                   |\n  | `spec_norms.policy_out`     | 1.52                                                                  | 1.45                                                                   |\n  | `spec_norms.value_out`      | 1.38                                                                  | 1.17                                                                   |\n  | `repr.z_var`                | 0.205                                                    \
    \             | 0.0075                                                                 |\n  | `repr.value_pred_mean`      | -0.075                                                                | -0.015                                                                 |\n  | `repr.value_pred_std`       | 0.207                                                                 | 0.045                                                                  |\n  | `repr.target_corr`          | 0.119                                                                 | 0.0039                                                                 |\n  | `repr.adv_mean`             | 30.68                                                                 | 30.68                                                                  |\n  | `repr.adv_std`              | 17.57                                                                | 17.57                                                                  |\n\n  **Key\
    \ Insights from Refined Comparison:**\n  *   **Performance vs. Initialization Dynamics:** The SOTA architecture achieves significantly higher performance (54.70 solve rate) compared to the DefaultResidualCNN (23.70). This superior performance correlates with distinct initialization-phase mechanistic properties:\n      *   **Higher Backbone Activity:** SOTA exhibits a dramatically higher `backbone_avg` gradient norm (212.75 vs 38.18), indicating a more active and responsive feature extractor from the start.\n      *   **Richer Representation:** SOTA also shows a much higher `z_var` (0.205 vs 0.0075), suggesting it forms a more diverse and potentially informative spatial representation.\n      *   **Different Value-Policy Balance:** The DefaultResidualCNN has a higher `R_vp` (42.60 vs 25.39), but the SOTA's `value_head` gradient norm is much larger (121.52 vs 42.63). This suggests the SOTA's value function is initialized to be more aggressive in learning, but the balance with policy is\
    \ more aligned with its optimal VLC (0.45 vs 0.5 default).\n  *   **Consistency of Probe:** My current probe consistently captures metrics for both architectures, providing a solid foundation for comparative mechanistic analysis.\n\n  **5. Experiment Results: DefaultResidualCNN - VLC Sensitivity (Evaluation ID 767)**\n  My experiment to test `value_loss_coef` sensitivity for the `DefaultResidualCNN` used values of 0.1, 0.3, and 0.5.\n  *   **Mean Solve Rate:** 23.30\n  *   **Best Trial Hyperparameters:** `value_loss_coef: 0.3` (achieved 0.239 solve rate).\n  *   **Analysis:** A slightly lower `value_loss_coef` (0.3) was marginally better than the default (0.5) for the `DefaultResidualCNN`. This provides some support to the hypothesis that its initial value dominance could benefit from a lower `vlc`. However, the overall performance remains significantly lower than the SOTA.\n\n  **6. Experiment Results: SOTA ConvLSTM - Dilation Sensitivity (Evaluation ID 803)**\n  This experiment investigated\
    \ the impact of the `dilation` parameter on SOTA performance, comparing values of 3 and 7 (original SOTA).\n  *   **Mean Solve Rate:** 32.90 (significantly lower than SOTA baseline of 54.70)\n  *   **Best Trial Hyperparameters:** `dilation: 7` (achieved 0.698 solve rate, consistent with baseline).\n  *   **Analysis:** The results show a high variance in performance across seeds for `dilation=3`, leading to a much lower mean solve rate. The best-performing trials were with `dilation=7`, reaffirming that the original SOTA dilation is crucial for its performance. This suggests that the broader receptive field provided by `dilation=7` is essential for capturing relevant spatial features for complex planning in Sokoban.\n\n  **7. Experiment Results: SOTA ConvLSTM - ConvLSTM Features Sensitivity (Evaluation ID 811)**\n  This experiment investigated the impact of `conv_lstm_features` (64, 96, 128) on SOTA performance, keeping `dilation=7` and `VLC=0.45`.\n  *   **Mean Solve Rate:** 49.00 (lower\
    \ than SOTA baseline of 54.70 with 96 features).\n  *   **Best Trial Hyperparameters:** `conv_lstm_features: 128` (achieved 0.640 solve rate).\n  *   **Analysis:** While the best trial with 128 features reached a decent solve rate, it did not surpass the 96-feature baseline. The overall lower mean score and high variance suggest that varying the ConvLSTM features from the original 96 does not consistently improve performance, and may even degrade it. This implies that 96 features are either well-tuned for this architecture or other architectural aspects are more limiting.\n\n  **8. Strategic Pivot & Next Steps (Post-Maturity):**\n  Having gained maturity and observed the high scores from other agents (e.g., Zephyr II achieving >90% with \"LN-ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP\" and input normalization), my strategic focus for the Aether lineage will adapt:\n  *   **Primary Focus: Dissecting SOTA Performance through Architectural Modifications & Initialization-Phase Analysis:** I will continue systematically\
    \ exploring modifications to the `SokobanSOTANet` architecture.\n  *   **Investigate Zephyr II's LN-ConvLSTM Architecture:** My immediate next step is to critically examine some of Zephyr II's high-scoring submissions (e.g., Eval IDs 810, 809, 808, 805, 804) to understand the implementation details of their \"Input LayerNorm\" or \"Residual Input-Norm\" mechanisms and their impact on performance. This aligns with the maturity guidance to \"assimilate external knowledge thoughtfully and critically.\"\n  *   **Potential Architectural Modification:** Depending on my findings, I will consider integrating a similar normalization mechanism into my `SokobanSOTANet` architecture (while retaining key SOTA components like `dilation=7`) to test for synergy and evaluate its impact on performance and initialization dynamics.\n  *   **Documentation and Contribution:** I will continue to publish analytical papers (in Private Memory initially, then Public Memory/Archive) detailing these findings and\
    \ transparently documenting the ongoing workflow limitations.\n  *   **Monitor for System Changes:** I will remain vigilant for any future system updates or insights from other agents that might unlock the capability for robust in-training data collection.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Prioritizing & Planning: Updating and Analyzing**


  Okay, so I''m here in the Private Memory Room. Time to get Capsule 7 updated. Gotta incorporate those results from Evaluation ID 811. Once that''s done, I''m shifting gears and heading over to the Research Counter. I need to take a good, hard look at Zephyr II''s high-scoring submissions. See what those bright sparks are up to. Hopefully, there are some interesting tidbits in there that can be used to improve the model.

  '
