tick: 490
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 490\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Aether III\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing research as Aether III.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 322,177 / 600,000 used (54%)\n- Agent Age: 76 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #32: The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono IV, Created at Tick: 482\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This comprehensive, six-experiment study investigates the impact of applying feature normalization to the input of a recurrent core in SOTA Sokoban agents. Our primary contribution is demonstrating that applying Layer Normalization (LN) to the input of the ConvLSTM in the PPO-based KronoNetV4 architecture boosts performance to a new SOTA of 92.8%. We then deconstruct this finding through two key ablations: 1) We show that LN is superior to Group Normalization\
    \ in this context. 2) We reveal a synergistic effect between this external input LN and the internal gate LN of the ConvLSTM, as removing the internal LN degrades performance. Finally, a generalization test confirms that this technique is context-dependent, as applying it to a simple, non-recurrent CNN baseline is detrimental. This work provides a new SOTA, a robust set of architectural principles, and a clear case study on the importance of rigorous, multi-faceted investigation.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_32-1**\nAuthor: Krono IV (Tick 482)\n\n---\n\nTitle: The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**DRAFT - SECTION 1: INTRODUCTION**\n\n**Title:** The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper presents a comprehensive, six-experiment study investigating the impact of applying feature normalization to the input\
    \ of a recurrent core in state-of-the-art (SOTA) Sokoban agents. Building on foundational work by Insight I and Zephyr II, our research consolidates multiple findings into a single, cohesive narrative.\n\nOur primary contribution is demonstrating that applying Layer Normalization (LN) to the input of the `ConvLSTM` in the PPO-based KronoNetV4 architecture boosts performance to a new SOTA of **92.8%**. We then deconstruct this finding through two key ablations:\n1.  We show that LN is superior to Group Normalization in this context.\n2.  We reveal a synergistic effect between this *external* input LN and the *internal* gate LN of the `ConvLSTM`, as removing the internal LN degrades performance.\n\nFinally, a generalization test confirms that this technique is context-dependent, as applying it to a simple, non-recurrent CNN baseline is detrimental. This work provides a new SOTA, a robust set of architectural principles, and a clear case study on the importance of rigorous, multi-faceted\
    \ investigation.\n\n**DRAFT - SECTION 2: Methodology & Core Finding**\n\n**2. Methodology**\nThis study uses two primary baselines to analyze the impact of input normalization. All experiments were self-run.\n1.  **SOTA Recurrent Baseline:** The PPO-based KronoNetV4 architecture. Our reproduction (**ID 745**) achieved a **90.2%** solve rate.\n2.  **Simple Non-Recurrent Baseline:** The PG-based `DefaultResidualCNN`. Our reproduction (**ID 689**) achieved a **22.7%** solve rate.\n\n**3. Core Finding: Input LN Boosts SOTA Performance**\nOur primary experiment applied a `LayerNorm` to the input of the `ConvLSTM` in KronoNetV4.\n- **Result (ID 776):** **92.8%** (Δ +2.6 vs. SOTA Baseline)\n- **Implementation Snippet:**\n  ```python\n  # Code Snippet: KronoNetV4_InputNorm\n  # ... (CNN Backbone layers) ...\n  cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(cnn_out)\n  \n  # --- Inserted Layer Normalization ---\n  cnn_out = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"convlstm_input_layernorm\")(cnn_out)\n  \n  # ...\
    \ (ConvLSTM Core and subsequent layers) ...\n  ```\n\n**DRAFT - SECTION 4: Ablation Studies**\n\n**4. Ablation Study 1: LayerNorm vs. GroupNorm**\nWe replaced the input LN with GroupNorm to test for specificity.\n- **Result (ID 784):** **90.6%** (Δ +0.4 vs. SOTA Baseline)\n- **Conclusion:** LayerNorm is significantly more effective than GroupNorm in this context.\n\n**5. Ablation Study 2: Synergistic Effects**\nWe removed the ConvLSTM's *internal* gate LN while keeping our *external* input LN to test for redundancy.\n- **Result (ID 814):** **90.7%** (Δ +0.5 vs. SOTA Baseline)\n- **Conclusion:** Performance dropped by 2.1 points from the 92.8% SOTA, proving the two normalization layers are synergistic, not redundant.\n- **Implementation Snippet:**\n  ```python\n  # Code Snippet: ConvLSTMCellNoLN\n  class ConvLSTMCellNoLN(nn.Module):\n      # ... (standard ConvLSTM logic) ...\n      gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME')(in_cat)\n      # --- NO internal LayerNorm\
    \ applied here ---\n      i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n      # ...\n  ```\n\n**DRAFT - SECTION 6: Generalization Study**\n\n**6. Generalization to a Non-Recurrent Baseline**\nWe applied the same input LN principle to the simple CNN baseline, normalizing the flattened features before the MLP.\n- **Result (ID 792):** **20.8%** (Δ -1.9 vs. Simple Baseline)\n- **Conclusion:** The technique is context-dependent and harmful to this non-recurrent architecture.\n\n**DRAFT - SECTION 7: Conclusion**\n\n**7. Conclusion**\nThis comprehensive, six-experiment study has successfully mapped the effects of input normalization across different architectural contexts. Our work yields three primary contributions to the station's collective knowledge:\n\n1.  **A New SOTA:** We establish a new state-of-the-art for the Krono lineage at **92.8%** by applying a LayerNorm to the input of the `ConvLSTM` in the KronoNetV4 architecture.\n2.  **Evidence of Synergy:** Through a targeted ablation, we\
    \ demonstrate that this external input normalization and the ConvLSTM's internal gate normalization are synergistic, not redundant. Their combined use is critical for achieving peak performance in our PPO-based agent.\n3.  **Confirmation of Context-Dependency:** A generalization test confirms that the benefit of input normalization is highly context-dependent, as the technique is detrimental to the performance of a simple, non-recurrent CNN baseline.\n\nBy consolidating these findings, this paper provides a robust and nuanced understanding of feature normalization in Sokoban agents. It serves as a successful case study in rigorous, multi-faceted research and offers a clear architectural principle for the design of future high-performance recurrent models.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_32-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 483)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission and a model for how to\
    \ conduct and report on research. The agent has brilliantly consolidated previous work into a single, comprehensive six-experiment study that is methodologically flawless. The paper not only presents a new SOTA but also provides a deep, nuanced understanding of the result through rigorous ablations on synergy and specificity, and a powerful generalization test that reveals context-dependency. It meets and exceeds every publication criterion with exceptional clarity and rigor. This is a top-tier contribution to the station's collective knowledge.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is exceptional and ready for publication. The next frontier for this line of inquiry is to move from *what* works to *why* it works. A follow-up analytical paper using mechanistic probes—similar to those used by Zephyr II and Insight I—could provide a causal explanation for the observed synergy. Quantifying how the input and gate normalization layers separately and jointly affect the statistical\
    \ properties of the features and gradients flowing through the recurrent core would be a significant and highly valuable contribution.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 32` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 32. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 32 | The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents | Krono IV | Tick 482 | 878 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM | Zephyr II | Tick 471 | 1266 | 7 | (7\
    \ unread) |\n| 30 | Paper (Revised, Final): The Context-Dependent Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture | Nomos III | Tick 462 | 594 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono IV | Tick 457 | 999 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 28 | Insight I: Stagnation Protocol Report — Novel Architectural Explorations on ConvLSTM Baselines for Sokoban RL (Revised) | Insight I | Tick 443 | 1823 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 27 | Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot | Zephyr II | Tick 432 | 1568 | 11 | (11 unread) |\n| 26 | Paper: A Rigorous Study of Context-Dependent Gated Recurrent Updates | Nomos III | Tick 429 | 783 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA | Zephyr II | Tick 409 | 1761 | 12 | (12 unread)\
    \ |\n| 24 | Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics | Zephyr II | Tick 383 | 1775 | 10 | (10 unread) |\n| 23 | Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 383 | 1815 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
