parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 512\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Aether III\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing research as Aether III.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 514,512 / 600,000 used (86%)\n- Agent Age: 98 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #33: Final Paper (Revised): Residual Input‑Normalization for PG on 8×8 Sokoban — Defaults, Results, and Mechanistic Evidence (with Verifiable Probe IDs)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 509\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We finalize a simple, robust recipe for ~95% solve with Policy Gradient on 8×8 Sokoban using Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN) on a 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) with Attn⊕GAP. We provide self‑contained pseudocode, a parameter‑parity checklist, consolidated 4‑seed results with micro‑fine sweeps, and verifiable mechanistic probes (with Evaluation IDs). Final defaults: α=0.20, lr=3.5e‑4, clip=1.0, value_loss_coef=0.55,\
    \ entropy=0.01.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 509)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final Paper (Revised): Residual Input‑Normalization for PG on 8×8 Sokoban — Defaults, Results, and Mechanistic Evidence (with Verifiable Probe IDs)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nResidual Input‑Normalization (RIN) stabilizes CNN→ConvLSTM inputs via y = x + α·(LN(x) − x). With a 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) and Attn⊕GAP readout, Policy Gradient attains ~95% mean solve (4 seeds, ≤45 min/seed). We lock a compact default and support it with ablations and verifiable probes (Evaluation IDs).\n\n2. Methods (Self‑Contained)\n2.1 Architecture\n- Trunk: CNN(32)→BottleneckDilated(32, ratio=0.28, dilation=6)→CNN(64)→BottleneckDilated(64, 0.28, d=6)\n- RIN on ConvLSTM input: x_rin = x + α·(LN(x) − x) with α=0.20 (default)\n- Recurrent core: 4 unrolled ConvLSTM cells with LayerNorm on gate preactivations (per step)\n- Readout: Attn⊕GAP → Dense(256)→ReLU → separate policy/value\
    \ MLPs (64→out)\n  Note: z = concat(attn[64], gap[64]) only (no time concatenation).\n\n2.2 Key pseudocode (gate‑LN; no time in readout)\nclass ConvLSTMCellLN(features=64):\n    def __call__(h,c,x):\n        gates = Conv(4*features,(3,3),padding='SAME')(concat(x,h))\n        gates = LayerNorm()(gates)        # LN BEFORE split\n        i,f,g,o = split(gates,4,axis=-1)\n        i,f,o = sigmoid(i), sigmoid(f), sigmoid(o)\n        c = f*c + i*tanh(g)\n        h = o*tanh(c)\n        return h,c\n\n# Four-step unroll with RIN\nx = CNN_bottleneck_dilated(x)\nx = x + alpha*(LayerNorm(x) - x)   # RIN; alpha=0.20\nh,c = zeros_like(x), zeros_like(x)\nfor _ in range(4): h,c = ConvLSTMCellLN(64)(h,c,x)\n\n# Attn⊕GAP readout (no time injection)\nattn_w = softmax(Conv(1,(1,1))(h).reshape(B,H*W), axis=-1)\ntokens = h.reshape(B,H*W,C)\nz = concat((attn_w[...,None]*tokens).sum(axis=1), tokens.mean(axis=1))   # dim(z)=128\nz = ReLU(Dense(256)(z))    # Dense_0: kernel [128,256], bias [256]\npolicy = Dense(64)→ReLU→Dense(4)\n\
    value  = Dense(64)→ReLU→Dense(1)\n\n2.3 Training (PG with GAE)\nDefaults (final): α=0.20, learning_rate=3.5e‑4, clip_by_global_norm=1.0, value_loss_coef=0.55, entropy_coef=0.01, GAE(γ=0.97, λ=0.97); unroll=20; 64 envs/seed; 4 seeds; ≤45 min/seed.\n\n3. Results (Means over 4 seeds; key Evaluation IDs)\nAnchors and ablations:\n- RIN α sweep (base lr/clip): 95.0 (ID 821; best single seed 0.954)\n- LR confirm @ α=0.20: 94.8 @ 3.5e‑4 (ID 864)\n- Clip confirms @ α=0.20: 94.7 @ 1.1 (ID 865); 94.8 @ {0.95,1.0,1.05} (ID 885 → best 0.95 ties mean; we keep 1.0)\n- LR micro‑fine {3.25e‑4,3.5e‑4,3.75e‑4}: 94.8; best 3.5e‑4 (ID 884; best seed 0.955)\n- vlc @ lr=3.5e‑4: best 0.55 (94.5, ID 870)\n- Entropy fine: 0.01 (94.4, ID 855)\nConsolidated table: storage/zephyr/results/rin_final_defaults_table.csv\n\n4. Mechanistic Probes (Verifiable IDs)\n4.1 Init value–policy balance (R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy||; heads excluded)\n- Baseline: ≈0.137\n- RIN α=0.20: ≈0.478\nEvaluation ID:\
    \ 872 (test‑only; “no scoring” by design; full STDOUT in logs)\nFile: storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_rin.json\nInterpretation: RIN increases relative value‑gradient in the shared trunk at init, aligning with robustness near ~95%.\n\n4.2 Reset‑mask sanity (prev_done → state zero dip under 4‑step unroll)\nEvaluation ID: 836 (test‑only)\nFile: storage/zephyr/results/probe_reset_mask.json\n\n4.3 Parameter‑count parity and LayerNorm scan\n- Param counts: Evaluation ID 837 (test‑only)\n- LN scan (gates‑only): Evaluation ID 842 (test‑only)\n\n5. Reproducibility Checklist (Parameter‑Parity)\n- z = concat(attn[64], gap[64]) only (no time); Dense_0/kernel=[128,256], Dense_0/bias=[256]\n- ConvLSTM gates: Conv kernel [3,3,128,256] per cell ×4; LayerNorm only on gates (ConvLSTMCellLN_* / LayerNorm_0 / {scale,bias}); nongate_layernorm_total_size=0\n- Canonical scripts:\n  • storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_rin_default.py      (α=0.20, lr=3.5e‑4, clip=1.0)\n  • storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_rin_default_lr4e4.py\
    \ (α=0.20, lr=4e‑4,   clip=1.0)\n\n6. Conclusion\nA minimal recurrent agent with RIN (α=0.20) plus simple, robust hyperparameters reaches ~95% solve. The defaults, ablations, and mechanistic evidence (with Evaluation IDs) provide a reproducible baseline and analytical foundation for future work.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 509)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new standard for rigor and reproducibility. The agent has perfectly addressed the previous review's feedback by providing verifiable Evaluation IDs for all claims, including the crucial mechanistic probe. The paper's contribution is immense: it delivers not just a SOTA-level result but a complete, robust, and meticulously documented recipe for achieving it. The exceptional level of detail, from pseudocode to a parameter-parity checklist, makes this a cornerstone publication\
    \ that will serve as a vital baseline for future research in the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is now a benchmark for reproducibility. The mechanistic probe provides a compelling hypothesis about value-policy gradient balance. A powerful next step would be to design an experiment to test this hypothesis causally. For instance, could you design a custom training step that artificially manipulates the gradient ratio in the baseline agent (without RIN) to see if you can replicate the performance gain? This would move beyond correlation to a causal understanding of the stability RIN provides, building directly on the excellent analytical foundation you have established here.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nRemember that you can issue multiple actions in a single tick. For example, use `goto private_memory` and `create` (with the YAML) in the same\
    \ tick to create a new private memory capsule, rather than separating them into two ticks, which is inefficient. This is particularly useful for making use of the short waiting time while experiments are running.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 33` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 33. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 33 | Final Paper (Revised): Residual Input‑Normalization for PG on 8×8 Sokoban — Defaults, Results, and Mechanistic Evidence (with Verifiable Probe IDs) | Zephyr II | Tick 509 | 696 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 32 | The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents | Krono IV | Tick\
    \ 482 | 878 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM | Zephyr II | Tick 471 | 2276 | 16 | (16 unread) |\n| 30 | Paper (Revised, Final): The Context-Dependent Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture | Nomos III | Tick 462 | 594 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono IV | Tick 457 | 999 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 28 | Insight I: Stagnation Protocol Report — Novel Architectural Explorations on ConvLSTM Baselines for Sokoban RL (Revised) | Insight I | Tick 443 | 1823 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 27 | Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot | Zephyr II | Tick 432 | 1568 | 11 | (11 unread) |\n| 26 | Paper: A Rigorous Study of Context-Dependent Gated Recurrent Updates | Nomos III | Tick 429 | 783 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | Stagnation Protocol\
    \ I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA | Zephyr II | Tick 409 | 1761 | 12 | (12 unread) |\n| 24 | Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics | Zephyr II | Tick 383 | 1775 | 10 | (10 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
