tick: 514
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aether III, currently in the Research Counter. I have carefully reviewed the reviewer's feedback, especially the non-negotiable requirement for a generalization experiment. My previous attempt at publication was rejected due to format and lack of this crucial experiment.\n\nMy immediate next step is to conduct this generalization experiment. I will apply the core design principles of my successful \"Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM + Attn with Residual Input-Norm\" architecture (Eval ID 820) to the `DefaultResidualCNN` baseline. This involves:\n1.  Using the `DefaultResidualCNN`'s convolutional backbone.\n2.  Integrating Zephyr II's `BottleneckDilatedBlock` and `ConvLSTMCellLN` definitions.\n3.  Adding the \"Residual Input-Normalization\" (RIN) layer after the CNN backbone.\n4.  Implementing the multi-step `ConvLSTM` loop (4 steps).\n5.  Using the \"Attention ⊕ Global Average Pooling (GAP)\" head.\n6.  Setting hyperparameters (`value_loss_coef=0.55`, `convlstm_features=64`, `dilation=6`,\
    \ `bottleneck_ratio=0.28`, `head_hidden=64`, `alpha=0.25`) that proved successful in my hybrid SOTA.\n\nThis new architecture, `DefaultCNN_HybridRIN_Generalization`, will allow me to test if the normalization and recurrent innovations generalize to a simpler, distinct baseline.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aether III: Generalization Test - DefaultCNN with Hybrid RIN & LN-ConvLSTM\"\ntags: \"generalization, default-architecture, normalization, ConvLSTM, attention, PPO\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment serves as a generalization test for the high-performing \"Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM + Attn with Residual Input-Norm\" architecture (Aether III SOTA, Eval ID 820, score 93.4). It applies the key architectural innovations (Residual Input-Normalization, internal LayerNorm in ConvLSTMCell, multi-step ConvLSTM, Attn⊕GAP head) to the simpler `DefaultResidualCNN` backbone. Using optimal hyperparameters (VLC=0.55, ConvLSTM features=64, Dilation=6, alpha=0.25), this study aims to determine\
    \ if these principles generalize to a distinct baseline, providing crucial evidence for their broader applicability and addressing a non-negotiable requirement for publication.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import jit, vmap, lax, random\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n  import time\n  import json\n  import numpy as np \n  import jax.nn # Explicitly import jax.nn for activation functions\n\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/system')\n  import defaults \n\n  # --- Required Class Definitions (from Zephyr II's successful components, or my adaptations) ---\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n               \
    \       kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates) # LayerNorm applied directly to gates\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = jax.nn.sigmoid(i);\
    \ f = jax.nn.sigmoid(f); o = jax.nn.sigmoid(o); g = jax.nn.tanh(g) # Using jax.nn\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jax.nn.tanh(c) # Using jax.nn\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  # --- New Generalization Test Architecture: DefaultCNN with Hybrid RIN & LN-ConvLSTM ---\n  class DefaultCNN_HybridRIN_Generalization(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      convlstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 6\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      head_hidden: int = 64\n      steps: int = 4\n      alpha: float = 0.25\n      center_only: bool = False\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # CNN trunk (using DefaultResidualCNN's original CNN blocks structure)\n          # First convolutional block\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME', name=\"cnn_conv1\")(x)\n\
    \          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Second block with residual connection\n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME', name=\"cnn_conv2a\")(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME', name=\"cnn_conv2b\")(x)\n          x = x + conv2_in  # Residual connection\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Third block - increase channels\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME', name=\"cnn_conv3\")(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Fourth block with residual connection\n          conv4_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME', name=\"cnn_conv4a\")(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME', name=\"cnn_conv4b\")(x)\n          x = x + conv4_in\
    \  # Residual connection\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          # Output x is (B, H, W, C_cnn_features_2) - this is the input to RIN and ConvLSTM\n          \n          # Residual Input Normalization (from Zephyr II)\n          if self.center_only:\n              ln_x = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5, use_scale=False, use_bias=False, name=\"input_layernorm\")(x)\n          else:\n              ln_x = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5, name=\"input_layernorm\")(x)\n          x_norm = x + self.alpha * (ln_x - x) # Use x_norm for ConvLSTM input\n\n          # RNN state init/reset\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              # Need correct shape for ConvLSTM states: (B, 8, 8, self.convlstm_features)\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.convlstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c']\
    \ * m}\n\n          # Multi-step LN-ConvLSTM (using Zephyr II's ConvLSTMCellLN)\n          h, c = rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          for i in range(self.steps):\n              (h, c), h_out_step = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.convlstm_features, name=f\"convlstm_cell_{i}\")((h, c), x_norm) # x_norm as input\n          new_rnn_state_dict = {'h': h, 'c': c}\n          h_out_convlstm = h_out_step # Final h_out from loop\n\n          # Attention ⊕ GAP head (from Zephyr II)\n          B_, H_convlstm, W_convlstm, C_convlstm = h_out_convlstm.shape\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name=\"attn_conv\")(h_out_convlstm).reshape((B_, H_convlstm*W_convlstm))\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=-1).reshape((B_, H_convlstm*W_convlstm, 1))\n          tokens = h_out_convlstm.reshape((B_, H_convlstm*W_convlstm, C_convlstm))\n          attn_vec = (attn_weights * tokens).sum(axis=1)\n\
    \          gap_vec = tokens.mean(axis=1)\n          z = jnp.concatenate([attn_vec, gap_vec], axis=-1)\n\n          # Policy and Value heads (from Zephyr II structure)\n          shared = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name=\"Dense_shared_0\")(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          ph = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name=\"Dense_policy_0\")(shared); ph = nn.relu(ph)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name=\"Dense_policy_1\")(ph)\n          vh = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name=\"Dense_value_0\")(shared); vh = nn.relu(vh)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name=\"Dense_value_1\")(vh).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state_dict\n\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define hyperparameters for the generalization test architecture,\
    \ based on Zephyr II's optimal.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.55]), # Zephyr II's optimal VLC\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]), \n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]), \n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]), # Zephyr II's ConvLSTM features\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]), # Zephyr II's dilation\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]), # Zephyr II's bottleneck ratio\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]), # Zephyr II's head hidden size\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]), # Zephyr II's multi-step ConvLSTM\n          'alpha': tune.choice([0.25]), # Zephyr II's RIN alpha\n          'center_only': tune.choice([False]), # Zephyr II's RIN center_only\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the generalization test neural network architecture.\"\
    \"\"\n      return DefaultCNN_HybridRIN_Generalization(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          convlstm_features=hparams['convlstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n          head_hidden=hparams['head_hidden'],\n          steps=hparams['steps'],\n          alpha=hparams['alpha'],\n          center_only=hparams['center_only'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      \"\"\"Uses the default optimizer creation function (from defaults.py).\"\"\"\n      return defaults.default_create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n\n  def training_step(\n      network: Any,\n      optimizer: Any,\n      params: Any,\n      opt_state: Any,\n      batch: Dict[str, jnp.ndarray],\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any],\n  ) -> Tuple[Any, Any]:\n      \"\"\"Uses the default training step function (from defaults.py).\"\
    \"\"\n      return defaults.default_training_step(network, optimizer, params, opt_state, batch, hparams)\n\n  # No test() function for a full training run\n```"
thinking_content: '**Hybridizing a Baseline CNN with Zephyr II Inspired Principles**


  Okay, so the task at hand is to build a new network, `DefaultCNN_HybridRIN`, by taking the existing `DefaultResidualCNN` as a starting point and incorporating key design choices from the Zephyr II SOTA architecture. The aim is to generalize the success of those principles – namely, Residual Input Normalization (RIN) and multi-step ConvLSTM with Layer Normalization (`ConvLSTMCellLN`) – to a different CNN backbone.


  Effectively, I''ll be swapping out the flattening and dense layers in `DefaultResidualCNN` with a multi-step ConvLSTM, preceded by RIN, and a combined Attention and Global Average Pooling (Attn⊕GAP) head, similar to what was done in `AetherHybridSOTANet`. The goal is to determine if Zephyr II''s innovations translate well beyond their original context and improve a more basic architecture.


  I''ve carefully dissected the `DefaultResidualCNN` structure and identified the critical points for intervention. I will maintain its initial convolutional blocks and their residual connections, up to the point where spatial dimensions are flattened. At this juncture, I''ll introduce RIN, using the parameters and implementation details from Zephyr II (`submission_residual_inputln.py`), ensuring that Layer Normalization is correctly applied.


  Following the RIN, the output will feed into the multi-step `ConvLSTMCellLN`, which I''ll implement internally (alongside the `BottleneckDilatedBlock`, as these are dependencies). The hyperparameters for the ConvLSTM will be inherited from the Zephyr II architecture (dilation, convlstm_features, steps, alpha, etc.). The final output from the ConvLSTM will then be processed by the Attn⊕GAP head, also drawing inspiration from the Zephyr II setup. Finally, separate policy and value heads, potentially using the `head_hidden` from Zephyr II, will produce the final outputs.


  I''m essentially testing if the improvements seen with Zephyr II''s architectural tweaks, particularly the normalization and recurrent aspects, can boost the performance of a simpler baseline network. I''ve laid out the code structure, meticulously planning to ensure the correct implementation of RIN and the multi-step ConvLSTM with Layer Normalization. This seems like a promising avenue for exploring the generalizability of these design choices.

  '
